  "text_md": "convergence tol 10−8 Stopping tolerance maxit 1000 Maximum number of restarts lsqrtol 10ǫ Stopping tolerance of lsqr lsqrmaxit 10n Maximum number of iterations of lsqr u1 generated by randn\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile2.png>)\n \n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile12.png>)\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile5.png>)\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile14.png>)\nmax(A) σmin(A) , available from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection, is the condition number of A and indicates the level of ill-conditioning of A and reﬂects magnitudes of generalized singular values of {A,L}. In order to ensure that all the A are ﬂat or square, i.e., m n, we transpose some matrices, denoted by superscript T. Note that the smallest singular value of kneser 8 3 1T is tiny, which causes that the matrix is numerically row\n...\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile7.png>)\n...\n3 1 1\nand then normalized\n \n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile13.png>)\n...\nGSVD components, where target > 0 or target < 0 means that the largest or smallest GSVD components are required.\n20 K. FANG AND Z. JIA\n7. Numerical experiments. We now present a number of numerical experiments to demonstrate the performance of Algorithm 6.1, written in MATLAB language, and compare it with the public C language code of TRJBD in [1]. The experiments will illustrate that Algorithm 6.1 is at least competitive with and can outperform TRJBD considerably in terms of restarts. Since section 4.4 has shown that each restart of IRJBD and TRJBD basically costs the same for the same l and kmax, fewer restarts mean higher eﬃciency. The experiments were performed on an AMD Ryzen 7 5800X CPU with 78 GB RAM and 8 cores using the MATLAB R2024a with the machine precision ǫ = 2.22 × 10−16 under the Ubuntu 20.04.3 LTS 64-bit system.\n![](<2503.04041_pg20_images/imageFile9.png>)",
